{"name":"Sweetest Damask","hex":"#a7bdba","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweetest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["superlative form of sweet: most sweet"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweetest"},{"term":"damask","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An ornate silk fabric originating from Damascus.","Linen so woven that a pattern is produced by the different directions of the thread, without contrast of colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish-pink colour, like that of the damask rose."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/damask"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sweetness","blocks":[{"text":"Sweetness is a basic taste most commonly perceived when eating foods rich in sugar. Sweet tastes are generally regarded as pleasurable. In addition to sugars like sucrose, many other chemical compounds are sweet, including aldehydes, ketones, and sugar alcohols. Some are sweet at very low concentrations, allowing their use as non-caloric sugar substitutes. Such non-sugar sweeteners include saccharin, aspartame, sucralose and stevia. Other compounds, such as miraculin, may alter perception of sweetness itself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Strawberry_shortcake.jpg/330px-Strawberry_shortcake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweetness"},{"title":"Damask","blocks":[{"text":"Damask is a woven, reversible patterned fabric. Damasks are woven by periodically reversing the action of the warp and weft threads. The pattern is most commonly created with a warp-faced satin weave and the ground with a weft-faced or sateen weave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/ItalianSilkDamask.jpg/330px-ItalianSilkDamask.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damask"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https